.TH _PRINTF 3 "March 2026" "1.0" "Custom Printf Library"

.SH NAME
_printf \- formatted output conversion

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"
.PP
.B int _printf(const char *\fIformat\fR, ...);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fB_printf()\fR function produces output according to a \fIformat\fR string. 
It writes output to \fBstdout\fR, the standard output stream.

The format string is composed of zero or more directives: ordinary characters 
(not \fB%\fR), which are copied unchanged to the output stream; and conversion 
specifications, each of which results in fetching zero or more subsequent arguments.

.SS Conversion Specifiers
The following conversion specifiers are supported:

.TP
.B %c
Prints a single character.

.TP
.B %s
Prints a string of characters.

.TP
.B %d, %i
Prints a signed decimal integer.

.TP
.B %u
Prints an unsigned decimal integer.

.TP
.B %o
Prints an unsigned octal integer.

.TP
.B %x
Prints an unsigned hexadecimal integer (lowercase).

.TP
.B %X
Prints an unsigned hexadecimal integer (uppercase).

.TP
.B %p
Prints a pointer address in hexadecimal.

.TP
.B %%
Prints a literal percent sign.

.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, \fB_printf()\fR returns the number of characters printed 
(excluding the null byte used to end output to strings).

On error, \fB-1\fR is returned if \fIformat\fR is NULL or if the format 
string ends with a single \fB%\fR.

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Print a string:
.PP
.RS
_printf("Hello, %s!\\n", "world");
.RE

.PP
Print an integer:
.PP
.RS
_printf("Number: %d\\n", 42);
.RE

.PP
Print in hexadecimal:
.PP
.RS
_printf("Hex: %x\\n", 255);
.RE

